const faq = [
  { q: 'Where does the Fan-tailed Raven live?', a: 'The Fan-tailed Raven (Corvus rhipidurus) is confined to arid rocky habitats from the Horn of Africa and Red Sea coast north through the Arabian Peninsula to the Levant and south-western Asia. Its range includes Somalia, Djibouti, Eritrea, Ethiopia (arid lowlands), Sudan (Red Sea Hills), Saudi Arabia, Yemen, Oman, UAE, Jordan, Israel (Negev, Judean Desert), Sinai, and south-eastern Turkey. It is essentially the cliff-dweller of the arid Red Sea and Arabian zone.' },
  { q: 'Is the Fan-tailed Raven found in Israel?', a: 'Yes. The Fan-tailed Raven is resident in the Negev Desert and Judean Desert of Israel, particularly around cliff faces and rocky gorges. Mitzpe Ramon and the Ramon Crater area are reliable observation spots. The species co-occurs here with the Brown-necked Raven — which uses flatter terrain — making the Fan-tailed Raven\'s cliff association the key habitat difference between them in this region.' },
  { q: 'Why is the Fan-tailed Raven so cliff-dependent?', a: 'The Fan-tailed Raven is an obligate cliff-nester throughout its range — it requires steep cliff faces for nesting and roosting. Its strong cliff association is likely driven by: (1) a predation-free nest site (cliff ledges and crevices are inaccessible to most mammalian predators); (2) the cliff micro-habitat supporting thermal updrafts ideal for the Fan-tailed Raven\'s short-tailed, broad-winged soaring flight; and (3) the cliff face providing shelter and cool microclimate in its otherwise brutally hot arid range.' },
];
