    <h1>Fan-tailed Raven Range: Where This Species Lives</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The Fan-tailed Raven (<em>Corvus rhipidurus</em>) is confined to arid cliff habitats from the Horn of Africa and Red Sea coast across the Arabian Peninsula to the Levant and south-western Turkey. It is an obligate cliff-nester, present wherever there are suitable rocky gorges and escarpments in its arid zone range. It is absent from the flat desert interior where it cannot nest.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Distribution</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The Fan-tailed Raven's range follows the Red Sea and Arabian zone of cliff habitat:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Horn of Africa:</strong> Somalia (northern highlands and cliff zones), Djibouti, Eritrea, Ethiopia (eastern lowland scarps and Rift Valley cliff zones — lower than the Thick-billed Raven's highland niche).</li>
      <li><strong>Red Sea coast:</strong> Sudan (Red Sea Hills), Eritrea coastal zone.</li>
      <li><strong>Arabian Peninsula:</strong> throughout Saudi Arabia (Hejaz Mountains and Asir escarpment especially), Yemen, Oman (Hajar Mountains), UAE (Hajar foothills), and extending to Kuwait and Iraq in the north.</li>
      <li><strong>Levant:</strong> Jordan, Israel (Negev, Judean Desert, Arava), Sinai Peninsula (Egypt), and reaching south-eastern Turkey in the Euphrates valley area.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The key to its distribution is not the desert itself but the availability of cliff faces within the desert — absent from flat terrain even within its geographic range.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Habitat</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Within its range, the Fan-tailed Raven is a cliff specialist:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Nesting:</strong> obligatory cliff-face nester — ledges and crevices on steep escarpments, gorge walls, and rocky outcrops in hot arid terrain.</li>
      <li><strong>Communal roosting:</strong> cliff faces used for communal roosting outside the breeding season; some roosts number dozens to hundreds of birds.</li>
      <li><strong>Foraging:</strong> forages away from cliffs in adjacent desert scrub, wadis, and human habitation; exploits food waste from Bedouin camps, urban areas, and rubbish dumps.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Fan-tailed Ravens and Brown-necked Ravens co-occur throughout much of the Arabian Peninsula and Levant. The Fan-tailed Raven's strict cliff association and the Brown-necked Raven's more flexible nest site use (cliff, palm, pylon) is the key ecological difference allowing coexistence.</p>
  </section>

